Mutation expansion — each, every, and compound terms

Runtime mutation arguments may use each and every wire references to expand one mutation into multiple ground facts before the transaction is committed.

Expansion happens in the mutation layer. The Logic engine receives only the resulting ordinary ground terms.

Quick reference

Form	Meaning

text each W	One value from vector W for each expansion row
number each W	One number from vector W for each expansion row
bool each W	One boolean from vector W for each expansion row
text list each W	One list from each matrix row
text every W	Every value in vector W for every expansion row
number every W	Every number in vector W for every expansion row
bool every W	Every boolean in vector W for every expansion row
text list every W	Every row-list in matrix W for every expansion row
no modifier	One value, broadcast to every expanded fact


each preserves the existing zip semantics. every introduces Cartesian expansion.


---

each — synchronized expansion

each associates a wire value with the current expansion row.

For example:

+ pair(text each owners, text each cars)

with:

owners = [a, b, c]
cars   = [h, i, j]

expands to:

pair(a, h)
pair(b, i)
pair(c, j)

Multiple each arguments are zipped together.

All each arguments must have the same row count. A mismatch causes the entire mutation transaction to fail.


---

every — Cartesian expansion

every treats every element of a collection as an alternative value.

For example:

+ pair(text every owners, text every cars)

with:

owners = [a, b, c]
cars   = [h, i]

expands to:

pair(a, h)
pair(a, i)
pair(b, h)
pair(b, i)
pair(c, h)
pair(c, i)

Unlike each, every does not participate in row zipping.

It behaves conceptually as though the values had been supplied individually:

a, b, c

and:

h, i

and all combinations were generated.


---

Combining each and every

each establishes the synchronized expansion rows. every is then expanded independently for each row.

Example:

+ triple(text each owners, text each cars, text every colors)

with:

owners = [a, b]
cars   = [h, i]
colors = [red, blue, green]

produces:

triple(a, h, red)
triple(a, h, blue)
triple(a, h, green)

triple(b, i, red)
triple(b, i, blue)
triple(b, i, green)

Therefore:

each + each → zip

every + every → Cartesian product

each + every → Cartesian product within each each row


A useful mental model is:

each  → choose the row
every → choose every value for that row


---

Broadcast values

Arguments without each or every are broadcast.

For example:

+ tag(text each owners, active, text list tags)

with:

owners = [a, b, c]
tags   = [red, blue]

produces:

tag(a, active, [red, blue])
tag(b, active, [red, blue])
tag(c, active, [red, blue])

The active atom and the complete tags list are reused for every expanded fact.


---

Compound terms

Expansion modifiers can occur inside compound terms.

The compound itself remains an ordinary Logic compound; its arguments are expanded structurally before mutation commit.

For example:

+ located(
    text each names,
    zone(number each ids, text each areas)
)

with:

names = [john, mary, joe]
ids   = [1, 2, 3]
areas = [north, south, east]

produces:

located(john, zone(1, north))
located(mary, zone(2, south))
located(joe, zone(3, east))

The expansion is therefore recursive through the term structure.


---

Compound with every

every can also occur inside a compound.

+ located(
    text each names,
    zone(number each ids, text every areas)
)

with:

names = [john, mary]
ids   = [1, 2]
areas = [north, south, east]

produces:

located(john, zone(1, north))
located(john, zone(1, south))
located(john, zone(1, east))

located(mary, zone(2, north))
located(mary, zone(2, south))
located(mary, zone(2, east))

The outer each values remain synchronized while every creates alternatives inside the compound.


---

Nested compounds

Expansion can be nested at multiple levels:

+ property(
    text each names,
    details(
        number each prices,
        rent(number each rents, text every colors)
    )
)

The resulting facts are ordinary ground compounds such as:

property(
    john,
    details(100, rent(10, red))
)

followed by the corresponding combinations for the other colors and rows.

The Logic engine does not need special knowledge of each or every; it receives the expanded terms.


---

Lists and compounds

Lists remain distinct from compounds in the Logic value model.

For example:

zone(1, north)

is a compound, while:

[1, north]

is a list.

A compound can contain lists:

+ car(text each owners, text list each carsMatrix)

For a matrix:

carsMatrix =
[
    [a, b],
    [c],
    [d, e, f]
]

the text list each argument produces one list value per row:

car(owner1, [a, b])
car(owner2, [c])
car(owner3, [d, e, f])

That list is then simply an argument of the resulting compound/fact.


---

Expansion and transactions

Expansion occurs before constraint validation and commit.

The runtime pipeline is:

wire references
      ↓
each / every expansion
      ↓
ground mutation operations
      ↓
constraint validation
      ↓
atomic commit

For example:

logic {
    + pair(text each owners, text every cars)
}

may produce many individual pair/2 facts.

If any resulting operation violates a constraint, the whole mutation transaction fails:

mutationFailed = 1

and none of the generated facts are committed.

There is therefore no partial commit of an expansion.


---

Expansion rules

Argument	Expansion

active	Broadcast one atom
text wire	Broadcast one decoded atom
text list wire	Broadcast one complete list
text each vector	One atom per row
text every vector	Every atom for every row
text list each matrix	One list per matrix row
text list every matrix	Every row-list for every expansion row


The same principle applies to number and bool where their corresponding wire forms are supported.


---

Summary

each and every intentionally have different roles:

each   = synchronized / zip expansion
every  = Cartesian / alternative expansion
plain  = broadcast

And because expansion is structural, the same semantics can be used inside compound terms without adding compound-specific mutation syntax.

This keeps mutation syntax compact while still allowing large relational datasets to be generated directly from vector and matrix wires.